# CODEOWNERS example for a repository in the custom product management runner loop.
#
# Copy this file to .github/CODEOWNERS in the target repository and edit the paths.
# It cannot live here and apply automatically: CODEOWNERS is NOT one of the default
# community health files GitHub inherits from a .github repository. Every repository
# needs its own copy.
#
# What this is for in the loop: CODEOWNERS is what makes "a human must look at this"
# enforceable. When a path here matches, GitHub requests review from the owner, and with
# "Require review from Code Owners" enabled in branch protection the builder identity
# cannot merge until that review exists.
#
# Bot identities must never be listed as owners. An owner is a person who can say no.

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                           @rusty-cole

# The loop's own control surfaces. A change here changes how every other change is made,
# so it always needs a person.
/.github/                   @rusty-cole
/.github/workflows/         @rusty-cole
/runner/              @rusty-cole
/temporal_worker/           @rusty-cole

# Anything that can move money, secrets, or production.
/deploy/                    @rusty-cole
/infra/                     @rusty-cole
**/secrets/                 @rusty-cole
*.pem                       @rusty-cole

# Generated evidence. Owned so a person notices if it is ever hand-edited, which would
# defeat the point of it being generated.
/receipts/                  @rusty-cole
